nation portion is not, the clean split above is the accurate clarification. If you want a definitive ruling on *which* upstream component owns ingestion/pagination, that agent should be consulted directly — I can only confirm it is not the HITL gate.\n\n```json\n{\n    "Name": "HITL Gate (Redaction Review)",\n    "Inquiry": "Clarify: for the customer support & DSAR log redaction task, does the HITL gate require per-page units produced by ingestion/pagination before it can build the review payload, and is the ingestion/pagination determination itself in scope for the HITL gate or elsewhere?",\n    "Mode": "Fulfill",\n    "Response": "Yes to the first part, no to the second. (1) The HITL gate requires per-page units from ingestion/pagination as a hard precondition: payload assembly is deterministic and executed one page at a time, and needs page_id, page_number, total_pages plus the unredacted text/highlights and the masked text_with_masks for that page. Without the upstream per-page unit there is nothing to assemble. (2) The ingestion/pagination determination is NOT in scope for the HITL gate. The HITL gate does not perform ingestion, pagination, detection, redaction, or learning; ingestion/pagination is owned upstream (the ingestion/pagination component). The gate only consumes the resulting per-page unit, presents the side-by-side review payload, halts in WAITING_FOR_HUMAN with no auto-advance, and captures the explicit human decision into the approval_matrix keyed by page_id. The downstream feedback-logging stage was consulted and confirmed it is not the owner of this inquiry; the ADD/REMOVE edit routing remains its role."\n}\n```'}
